    {
      "id": "iQPDrandT9DM9HBLBVmu2",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1086.0399050790995,
      "y": 920.0724692645147,
      "width": 1230.6591796875,
      "height": 1075,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b3V",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 189458390,
      "version": 3199,
      "versionNonce": 2028637526,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1782283577585,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "*** How to improve booking expereince\n-- Adding counter timer in UI as common count down technique to the time you have to complete the purchase\n-- Ensure ticket is clocked while user is checking out\n-- If user abandon the checkout process, ticket need to be released\n-- Implement distributed lock with TTL (using Redis)\n-- Why Redis?? not postgresSQL --> we need temporary reservation that can automatically expirses, native\nSQL does not support this. \n1. When user select ticket, acquire a lock in Reidis using unique identifer, with predefined TTL\n2. User complete purchaed, ticket's status in DB is marked as \"Book\", Redis lock is released manually\n3. If TTL expires, Redis automatically release the lock\n--> No recing condition in Redis, key is ticket value (ticketId)\n-- If our lock goes down, then user will experience the degrade. Double booking should still be handled, and prevented\nby DB using Optimistic Concurrency control (OCC) and row-level locking. User will get error wil trying to pay only\n-- If TTL expire during payment, DB transaction will fail in one of the user, we might reed issue refund.\n\n*** How API will scale\n1. Caching: caching read (on event name, performance bio, venue since these things rarely changes) \n---> Set up DB trigger to notify caching systems data has changed\n---> implement TTL policy for cache entries, ensure periodic refreshes. \n2. Load balancing\n-- Round robin or Least connections for event traffic distribution across server instance\n3. Horizontal scaling\n-- Event service is stateless, which allow to horizontally scale. \n\n\n*** ensure good user experience\n-- implement virtual waiting queue to manage user access during peak time\n-- user are placed in queue before being able to access booking page\n1. When user place in virtual queue, establish a connection (SSE or websocket), \nwith client, and add them to queue. Queue itself can be handled by Redis\n2. Periodically check and dequeue user from queue \n3. marked user as admitted in Redis, \n\n** Searching \n-- Full-text index in DB. (PostgresSQL has built-in full text search with tsvector and GIN index, MySQL is full-text indexing)\n-- Full text search engine like ElasticSearch\n----> Use CDC (change data capture) to sync data with SQL, for real-time/near-time data\n----> Enable fuzzy search functionality in ElasticSearch. (allow for error tolerances in query)\n-- To make search faster, can also implement edge caching/query result caching. For example,\nbeside Redis, can use CDN as cache result geographically closer to user, reduce latency, improve response time\n\n\n ",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "*** How to improve booking expereince\n-- Adding counter timer in UI as common count down technique to the time you have to complete the purchase\n-- Ensure ticket is clocked while user is checking out\n-- If user abandon the checkout process, ticket need to be released\n-- Implement distributed lock with TTL (using Redis)\n-- Why Redis?? not postgresSQL --> we need temporary reservation that can automatically expirses, native\nSQL does not support this. \n1. When user select ticket, acquire a lock in Reidis using unique identifer, with predefined TTL\n2. User complete purchaed, ticket's status in DB is marked as \"Book\", Redis lock is released manually\n3. If TTL expires, Redis automatically release the lock\n--> No recing condition in Redis, key is ticket value (ticketId)\n-- If our lock goes down, then user will experience the degrade. Double booking should still be handled, and prevented\nby DB using Optimistic Concurrency control (OCC) and row-level locking. User will get error wil trying to pay only\n-- If TTL expire during payment, DB transaction will fail in one of the user, we might reed issue refund.\n\n*** How API will scale\n1. Caching: caching read (on event name, performance bio, venue since these things rarely changes) \n---> Set up DB trigger to notify caching systems data has changed\n---> implement TTL policy for cache entries, ensure periodic refreshes. \n2. Load balancing\n-- Round robin or Least connections for event traffic distribution across server instance\n3. Horizontal scaling\n-- Event service is stateless, which allow to horizontally scale. \n\n\n*** ensure good user experience\n-- implement virtual waiting queue to manage user access during peak time\n-- user are placed in queue before being able to access booking page\n1. When user place in virtual queue, establish a connection (SSE or websocket), \nwith client, and add them to queue. Queue itself can be handled by Redis\n2. Periodically check and dequeue user from queue \n3. marked user as admitted in Redis, \n\n** Searching \n-- Full-text index in DB. (PostgresSQL has built-in full text search with tsvector and GIN index, MySQL is full-text indexing)\n-- Full text search engine like ElasticSearch\n----> Use CDC (change data capture) to sync data with SQL, for real-time/near-time data\n----> Enable fuzzy search functionality in ElasticSearch. (allow for error tolerances in query)\n-- To make search faster, can also implement edge caching/query result caching. For example,\nbeside Redis, can use CDN as cache result geographically closer to user, reduce latency, improve response time\n\n\n ",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    }
